WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.320
When a movie's main character makes a habit
of breaking the fourth wall and talking directly

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.380
to the audience, you know it'll have in-jokes
and Easter eggs crammed into every orifice.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.520
Such is the case with Deadpool, the R-rated
superhero action comedy set in 20th Century

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.630
Fox's X-Men-focused corner of the Marvel Universe.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.660
Here's a list of some of the best Easter eggs
that ol' Deadpool hid in his Hello Kitty backpack.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.849
Mild spoilers ahead…

00:00:27.849 --> 00:00:28.849
CREDITS WHERE IT'S DUE

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:33.430
The movie opens up with hilarious title cards,
setting the tone immediately.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.949
One gag comes in the form of a copy of People
magazine's Sexiest Man Alive 2010 issue fluttering

00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:40.340
across the screen.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.660
That issue, of course, gave Deadpool-star
Ryan Reynolds top sexiness honors.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.430
Moments later in the opening credits sequence,
we see a wallet flying through the air with

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.910
a picture of Green Lantern popping out of
it.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:57.160
As you probably know by now, Reynolds' career
took a nasty hit in 2011 when his starring

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.830
turn as the DC Comics character flopped at
the box office, killing the franchise before

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.260
it had the chance to take off.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.110
GHOSTS OF DEADPOOLS PAST

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:11.380
Deadpool features at least two amazing references
to Reynolds' past as the character.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.310
In one scene, Wade picks up an action figure
of Deadpool from 2009's X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:21.350
A little later, the villainous Ajax says he
wants to sew Wade's mouth shut.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.680
These are some pretty awesome and brazen shout-outs
to what most fans consider one of the worst

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.940
movie adaptations of a comic book character.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.930
Deadpool is known for his wisecracks and non-stop
talking.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.880
But in that Wolverine movie, his friggin'
mouth is sealed shut.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.890
MORE GREEN LANTERN SHADE

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.909
When the bad guys in the Workshop bring Wade
in to give him superpowers, he yells out that

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:45.130
he doesn't want his superhero suit to be green
or animated.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:49.880
That's yet another a dig at Green Lantern
from 2011, which featured Reynolds wearing

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.210
a CGI-superhero costume that…well, looked
like garbage.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.630
A MARROW VICTORY

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:01.040
This is one of the few Easter eggs that isn't
funny, but is definitely exciting for comic

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.040
fans.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.700
For a brief moment in the workshop, the camera
pans past a woman who seems to have a bunch

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.710
of bones sticking out of her back.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:12.620
This has got to be an oblique reference to
Marrow, a mutant whose power is that, uh,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.200
she has a bunch of bones sticking out of her
back.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.160
Which she uses to stab people.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.770
So while Deadpool has a pretty tenuous connection
to the X-Men franchise, seeing Marrow in this

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:24.060
movie is still pretty cool.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.150
PROOF IS IN THE PROF

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:30.580
When Colossus tries to drag Deadpool off to
the X-Men's mansion, Wade offers up one of

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.500
the best lines in the movie.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.360
Colossus says that they'll go see how the
Professor feels about his freeway murder spree.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.860
At that, Deadpool says, "McAvoy or Stewart?

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.360
These timelines are confusing!"

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.950
Of course, that's a reference to the fact
that X-Men: Days of Future Past gave the mutant

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:51.180
franchise a soft reboot, allowing actor James
McAvoy to take the role of Professor X from

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.560
Patrick Stewart.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.800
The fact that Deadpool pokes direct fun at
20th Century Fox's crazy continuity shenanigans

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.970
is yet another reason he's our hero.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:02.819
ON THE BLADE'S EDGE

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:07.910
When Ajax and Angel Dust head to the bar to
track down Wade, they run into his buddy Weasel,

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:12.040
who makes fun of their outfits and asks if
they're headed to watch Blade II.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.950
While that's a solid burn in and of itself,
the Blade franchise is actually another Marvel-based

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.340
property, and yet another example of Deadpool
breaking the fourth wall.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:26.260
Even better: Ryan Reynolds had a starring
role in Blade Trinity as Marvel Comics character

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.820
Hannibal King.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:31.340
Ryan Reynolds must really like comic books,
you guys.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.910
STAN AND STRIPPERELLA

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.540
It wouldn't be a Marvel movie without a cameo
from Stan Lee, the comic book writing legend

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.000
who's credited with co-creating the Marvel
universe as we know it.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.750
He hams it up as the DJ in the strip club
where Deadpool and Weasel go to find Vanessa.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.940
While Stan Lee's connection to the Deadpool
character is about as thin as it gets, his

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.300
connection to strippers is actually worth
mentioning.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:58.519
In 2003, he created a pretty lousy animated
show called Stripperella starring Pamela Anderson,

00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:02.890
which aired on Spike TV for one year before
getting axed.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:04.680
BUDGET CUTS?

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.470
Shortly before Deadpool opened in theaters,
one of the film's screenwriters, Rhett Reese,

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:14.590
revealed in an interview that the filmmakers
had 48 hours to cut over $7 million from its

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:18.349
budget before 20th Century Fox would give
it the greenlight.

00:04:18.349 --> 00:04:22.330
Fast forward to the scene in the movie where
Deadpool heads to the X-Men mansion to get

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:26.169
the help of Colossus and Negasonic Teenage
Warhead to rescue Vanessa.

00:04:26.169 --> 00:04:30.930
There, he mentions how it's strange that he
only ever sees the two of them…almost as

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.400
if there wasn't enough budget to get any more
X-Men characters to appear…

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.820
BOB!

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.870
While he's cutting up Ajax's goons before
the final showdown, Deadpool runs into his

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.289
old buddy Bob from his special forces days.

00:04:43.289 --> 00:04:47.830
Fans of the character know that, for a while,
a Hydra flunkie named Bob joined Deadpool

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.219
on lots of his comic book adventures.

00:04:50.219 --> 00:04:54.069
While Fox can't refer to Hydra (since the
rights to those characters live with Marvel

00:04:54.069 --> 00:04:58.900
Studios), the character's appearance is still
a nice nod to true comic diehards.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.479
"RHYMES WITH 'PULVERINE'"

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:06.389
There are so many references to Wolverine
in this movie, probably because of the aforementioned

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:07.919
X-Men Origins connection.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:11.830
We've got Deadpool telling the audience that
the movie got made because he provided "oral

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:14.449
relief" to someone whose name rhymes with
"Pulverine."

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:20.159
Later, in Deadpool's duffel bag of guns, there's
a copy of the People magazine issue from 2008

00:05:20.159 --> 00:05:22.919
that declared Hugh Jackman as the sexiest
man alive.

00:05:22.919 --> 00:05:27.860
Then, there's the bit at the end of the movie
where Vanessa pulls off Deadpool's mask…under

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.869
which she finds a magazine cut-out of Hugh
Jackman's face stapled to Wade's.

00:05:32.869 --> 00:05:36.259
SPECIAL THANKS (WITH TONGUE)

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:40.259
Towards the end of the credits, the movie
offers a "special thanks (with tongue)" to

00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:45.360
Rob Liefeld and Fabien Nicieza, the original
creators of the Deadpool character.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.650
Considering how Liefeld and Nicieza's creation
has taken over the pop culture world, they're

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:54.589
probably more than happy to accept that extra
special thanks, tongue and all.

00:05:54.589 --> 00:05:55.589
Thanks for watching!

00:05:55.589 --> 00:05:59.319
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to see more
videos like the one you just saw.

